  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero evidence about which modality, encoder, or fusion strategy is effective. The most fundamental uncertainty is whether multimodal fusion adds value over a single-modality baseline at all. 2) What this tests: config_012 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest meaningful RNA-only baseline—PCA introduces no learned parameters, single-profile avoids aggregation confounds, and omitting the image modality isolates RNA signal. It establishes a clean performance floor. 3) Why best under limited budget: A fast, well-understood baseline with minimal training confounds is the highest-information first experiment. It calibrates expected accuracy/F1, tells us whether RNA alone is competitive, and provides the reference against which all subsequent multimodal experiments must be judged. Without this anchor, any multimodal result would be uninterpretable. It is also computationally cheap, preserving budget for later, more complex experiments."
